      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{60-8x}$ at the point where $x=3.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=60-8x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-8.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{60-8x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{60-8x}})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{60-8(3)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
